[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":120},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1795048830000,"id","d59f54xd8aihc2yxqp79i5ec0","title","Amazon Cognito 分级认证指南：按操作风险配置 step-up authentication","originalTitle","Step-up authentication: how to right-size authentication to risk","summary","Amazon Cognito 支持按操作风险实施 step-up authentication，并通过 JWT 中的 acr、amr、auth_time 和 iat 声明记录认证强度、方法与时效。","reason","source",{"_72":119},"links",{"_61":118},"publishedAt","2026-11-18T23:49:07.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-18T23:49:36.758Z","timelineAt","category","blue-team","tags",[114,115,77,116,117],"score",45,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_79":80,"_13":81},"x","readingMode","full","author","Abrom Douglas","body",{"_110":-5,"_61":111,"_112":-5,"_113":36},"outline",[82,83,84,85,86,87,88,89],"relatedStories",[78],"topics",[68,69,70],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fyZrH_xX5eOo","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_71":76,"_72":77},{"_71":74,"_72":75},{"_71":30,"_72":73},"slug","name","蓝队与运营","tools","工具与平台","identity","身份与访问",{"_79":80,"_13":81},"publicId","62183041-93cb-41ba-b4a2-c9c541aa45a0","AWS介绍Amazon Cognito阶梯式认证",{"_11":108,"_91":109,"_93":97},{"_11":106,"_91":107,"_93":97},{"_11":104,"_91":105,"_93":97},{"_11":102,"_91":103,"_93":97},{"_11":100,"_91":101,"_93":97},{"_11":98,"_91":99,"_93":97},{"_11":95,"_91":96,"_93":97},{"_11":90,"_91":92,"_93":94},"sec-8","text","Abrom Douglas III","level",3,"sec-7","Conclusion",2,"sec-6","Pattern 4: Carrying assurance across a federated OIDC identity provider","sec-5","Pattern 3: Step-up on API and SDK sign-in","sec-4","Pattern 2: A portfolio of applications with different risk postures","sec-3","Pattern 1: Transaction-level step-up in a banking app","sec-2","How you request a level, and what stays your responsibility","sec-1","What ACR and AMR tell your application","zh","<p>Not every action a user takes in an application carries the same risk. Viewing an account balance in a banking application isn’t the same as wiring several thousand dollars to a new payee. In practice, though, many applications ask users to prove their identity once at sign-in and then treat every action the same from that point on, whether that sign-in used a password alone or included multi-factor authentication (MFA). That creates a tension: if the sign-in is strict enough for the most sensitive operation, everyday tasks carry unnecessary friction. If it’s easy enough for everyday tasks, the most sensitive operations might not be protected well enough. Even a strong sign-in from hours earlier says little about who is at the keyboard when a high-risk request arrives.</p><p><span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/\">Amazon Cognito</a></span> now resolves that trade-off by supporting step-up authentication, which records the authentication assurance level and the methods used, and including both in the JSON web tokens (JWTs) it issues. You ask for a target assurance level when a user signs in. Amazon Cognito returns the level the user reached in an <code>acr</code> claim (authentication context class reference) and the methods they completed in an <code>amr</code> claim (authentication methods reference). These claims are part of the <span><a href=\"https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html\">OpenID Connect Core specification</a></span>, so your application can reason about authentication strength in a standard way and match it to the potential risk of what the user is trying to do. In this post, I explain how the claims support step-up authentication and walk through four patterns that put them to use: transaction-level step-up in a banking app, varying risk postures across a portfolio of applications, step-up enforced on API and SDK sign-in, and authentication assurance carried across a federated third-party OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity provider.</p><p>You can request authentication levels through Amazon Cognito <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/authentication-flows-selection-managedlogin.html\">managed login</a></span> using the standard OIDC <code>acr_values</code> and <code>max_age</code> parameters, and through the SDK and API using the <code>USER_AUTH</code> flow with <code>TARGET_ACR_VALUES</code> and <code>MAX_AGE</code>. Requesting step-up authentication requires the Essentials or Plus <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-sign-in-feature-plans.html\">feature plan</a></span>. ; For the complete list of features, behaviors, and limitations, see the <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-step-up-authentication.html\">Authentication levels with ACR and AMR claims</a></span>.</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\"><b>What ACR and AMR tell your application</b></h2><p>Amazon Cognito defines four assurance levels, and the factor combinations that satisfy each one are fixed. You can use the default names as is or rename the level URIs to fit your organization’s own naming scheme. Renaming changes only the label. If you need to relate Amazon Cognito levels to a framework such as <span><a href=\"https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html#AAL_SEC4\">NIST SP 800-63</a></span> or <span><a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:02014R0910-20241018#art_8\">eIDAS</a></span>, make that mapping in your application. The following are the default Amazon Cognito defined levels.</p><ul> \n <li>Level 1 (<code>urn:cognito:loa:1</code>) is password only.</li> \n <li>Level 2 (<code>urn:cognito:loa:2</code>) is a single one-time password (OTP) over SMS or email.</li> \n <li>Level 3 (<code>urn:cognito:loa:3</code>) is a passkey on its own, or a password combined with an SMS or email OTP.</li> \n <li>Level 4 (<code>urn:cognito:loa:4</code>) is the highest: a password with a time-based one-time password (TOTP) code from an authenticator app.</li> \n</ul><p>Amazon Cognito assigns the highest level the user’s completed factors fully satisfy, so a user who signs in with a password and a TOTP code lands at level 4, even though they also met the assurance requirements for every level below it.</p><p>Four claims in the token facilitate a step-up decision. The <code>acr</code> claim is the level the sign-in reached and is what your application compares against what the operation requires. The <code>amr</code> claim lists the methods behind that level, so you can confirm a specific factor was used. The <code>auth_time</code> claim records when the user last authenticated interactively and is what <code>max_age</code> is measured against. The <code>iat</code> claim only records when the token was issued, so a refreshed token carries a new <code>iat</code> but keeps its old <code>auth_time</code>. Finally, send a nonce in the request and verify it on the returned ID token, so the result is tied to the request you made.</p><p>The following is an example access token for a user completing a level 4 authentication. Notice the four claims I’ve discussed: <code>acr</code>, <code>amr</code>, <code>auth_time</code>, and <code>iat</code>.</p><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">{ \n    \"sub\": \"387173a0-60c1-700a-4a02-e84b49bcc7a9\", \n    \"iss\": \"https://cognito-idp.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com/&lt;region&gt;_123abc\", \n    \"aud\": \"123abcn2ci4a99a4ieno123abc\", \n    \"exp\": 1445470140, \n    \"iat\": 499163700, \n    \"auth_time\": -446666160, \n    \"token_use\": \"access\", \n    \"acr\": \"urn:cognito:loa:4\", \n    \"amr\": [\"pwd\", \"otp\", \"mfa\"], \n    \"email\": \"marty@example.com\", \n    \"cognito:username\": \"387173a0-60c1-700a-4a02-e84b49bcc7a9\" \n}</code></pre><p>Two important behaviors are worth understanding before you build on this. First, a token refresh preserves the <code>acr</code> and <code>amr</code> values, so a user who reached level 4 keeps level 4 across refreshes and isn’t asked to prove it again on every rotation. Second, a refresh doesn’t reset the <code>auth_time</code> claim. That field records when the user interactively authenticated, not from a non-interactive token refresh. This is what lets you reason about freshness of authentication separately from assurance strength.</p><p>At a high level, every step-up interaction in this post follows the same loop: the application checks the token it already holds, redirects for a stronger level only when the operation requires more than the user has, and re-validates the <code>acr</code> claim on the freshly issued token before it proceeds. The following diagram provides a general step-up authentication flow.</p><p>\n <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/3bb9706a3a066ed6.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/d32e7a8db0226278.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/2250710b42867de2.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/3bb9706a3a066ed6.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Figure 1: General step-up authentication flow to access a sensitive operation\" width=\"912\" height=\"1069\">\n </p><p>Figure 1: General step-up authentication flow to access a sensitive operation</p><h2 id=\"sec-2\"><b>How you request a level, and what stays your responsibility</b></h2><p>The request side is deliberately straightforward. On managed login you add <code>acr_values</code> and optionally <code>max_age</code> to the or endpoint. In the SDK and API, you pass <code>TARGET_ACR_VALUES</code> and <code>MAX_AGE</code> in the <code>USER_AUTH</code> flow of the API. Both <code>acr_values</code> and <code>TARGET_ACR_VALUES</code> accept an ordered list of target levels. The same parameters work whether you’re setting the level for an initial sign-in or raising the level of a user who is already signed in; the patterns later in this post show both. Amazon Cognito acts only on what you send. It doesn’t infer the level you meant, so if you omit the parameter or request the wrong level, the user might not be stepped up the way you intended.</p><p>That points to the part your application and API owns: what assurance authentication level is needed. Amazon Cognito issues a token that honestly reports the level the user reached based on what your application requested. For example, deciding that a given operation requires an assurance level 3, checking the token’s <code>acr</code> against that requirement, and refusing the operation when it falls short is enforcement your application and API performs. Amazon Cognito produces the evidence; your code acts on it. In practice, that means checking the token you already hold before you redirect, so you don’t send a user to re-authenticate who already meets the bar, and validating the <code>acr</code> and <code>auth_time</code> claims on the tokens Amazon Cognito returns before you grant access. Amazon Cognito computes these claims itself, so you can’t customize, add to, or suppress them with a pre-token-generation Lambda trigger.</p><p>There is one more detail that saves debugging time later. If you rename your <code>acr</code> level URIs after tokens have already been issued, an older token might carry an <code>acr</code> value that no longer matches any configured level. Amazon Cognito treats that as an unrecognized level rather than an error. It still honors the token’s <code>amr</code> values and prompts only for factors not already present, without crediting any particular level. The <code>amr</code> list, in other words, remains meaningful even when a renamed level string goes stale. See <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-step-up-authentication.html#cognito-user-pools-step-up-acr-configuration\">Configuring ACR level names</a></span> to learn how to configure this.</p><p>With the claims and the division of responsibility in place, the following four patterns show how they work in practice. Each one applies the same loop from Figure 1 to a different situation: a single application, a portfolio of applications, API-driven sign-in, and federation.</p><h2 id=\"sec-3\"><b>Pattern 1: Transaction-level step-up in a banking app</b></h2><p>Consider a retail banking application. Signing in and viewing balances is routine, and the bank is comfortable requiring a moderate assurance level for it. Moving money is considered a privileged action, and a large transfer deserves stronger proof that the person at the keyboard is really the account holder.</p><p>The bank sets its baseline at level 2, so a user signs in with a one-time code over SMS or email and can see balances, statements, and pending transactions. When that same user initiates a transfer above $1,000, the application’s own policy says the operation requires level 4. Before it shows the transfer initiation screen, the application inspects the <code>acr</code> claim in the token it already holds. The user authenticated at level 2, level 2 is below the required level 4, so the application redirects the user to managed login with <code>acr_values=urn:cognito:loa:4</code>.</p><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">GET https://auth.example-bank.com/oauth2/authorize \n    ?response_type=code \n    &amp;client_id=abc123def456 \n    &amp;redirect_uri=https://app.example-bank.com/callback \n    &amp;scope=openid \n    &amp;acr_values=urn:cognito:loa:4</code></pre><p>Amazon Cognito now requires the user to complete a factor combination that satisfies level 4, for example entering their password and then a TOTP code from their authenticator app. When authentication finishes, Amazon Cognito issues fresh tokens with <code>acr</code> set to <code>urn:cognito:loa:4</code>, <code>amr</code> set to <code>[\"pwd\", \"otp\", \"mfa\"]</code>, and a fresh updated <code>auth_time</code> claim. The application validates that the new token meets its requirement and only then lets the transfer proceed. A transfer under the threshold never triggers any of this, so the everyday experience stays light and the friction lands exactly where the risk is.</p><p>The application logic reads naturally, here’s a pseudo JavaScript snippet:</p><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">// Use your own URIs here, if different than defaults.\nconst ACR_RANK = {\n  \"urn:cognito:loa:1\": 1,\n  \"urn:cognito:loa:2\": 2,\n  \"urn:cognito:loa:3\": 3,\n  \"urn:cognito:loa:4\": 4,\n};\nconst REQUIRED_LEVEL = \"urn:cognito:loa:4\";\nconst HIGH_VALUE_THRESHOLD = 1000;\n\nfunction canAuthorizeTransfer(accessTokenClaims, amount) {\n  if (amount &lt; HIGH_VALUE_THRESHOLD) {\n    return true; // baseline session is sufficient\n  }\n  // High-value transfer: require level 4.\n  // An unrecognized acr ranks 0, so it never passes.\n  const achieved = ACR_RANK[accessTokenClaims.acr] ?? 0;\n  return achieved &gt;= ACR_RANK[REQUIRED_LEVEL];\n}</code></pre><p>You can extend the same idea to freshness. A bank might accept a level 4 session for a high-value transfer only if the user authenticated within the last 5 minutes, which you express by adding <code>max_age=300</code> to the request. Amazon Cognito compares that window against the <code>auth_time</code> claim in the access token and reauthenticates the user if the window has passed, even if the token was refreshed seconds ago.</p><h2 id=\"sec-4\"><b>Pattern 2: A portfolio of applications with different risk postures</b></h2><p>Assurance levels are just as useful across a set of applications as they are within one. Suppose an organization runs five applications from a single Amazon Cognito user pool using managed login, and each one carries a different risk posture.</p><p>An internal cafeteria menu is low risk, so it’s fine with level 1, password only. An employee directory and an expense viewer hold personal data but nothing that moves money, so they require level 2: a one-time code over SMS or email on top of the password. A source code portal warrants a passkey or a password plus a one-time code, which is level 3. The production financial console is the crown jewel, so it demands level 4: a password with a TOTP code.</p><p>Each application requests its own floor by setting <code>acr_values</code> to the level that matches its risk, and each validates the <code>acr</code> claim on the returned token before granting access. Because the applications share a user pool, managed login keeps a single sign-on (SSO) session across them. A user who has already reached level 4 for the financial console is redirected to managed login when they open another application, finds the existing session, and receives tokens for that application without another prompt, provided the application doesn’t send a <code>max_age</code> that the session has outlived. Each application then compares levels by rank, so a level 4 token satisfies a level 2 floor. Someone who signed in at level 1 for the cafeteria menu, on the other hand, is stepped up the first time they open the expense viewer, because level 1 doesn’t meet the level 2 floor that the application enforces. The user pool stays single and central; the assurance requirement travels with each application’s own policy.</p><p>Renaming the level URIs helps here. If the organization already classifies systems against an internal assurance standard, it can set <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_CreateUserPool.html#CognitoUserPools-CreateUserPool-request-AcrConfiguration\">AcrConfiguration</a></span> on the user pool so that, say, level 3 reports as <code>urn:example-corp:assurance:standard</code> and level 4 as <code>urn:example-corp:assurance:high</code>. The factor combinations behind each level stay fixed; only the names change, so every application validates against strings its developers already recognize.</p><h2 id=\"sec-5\"><b>Pattern 3: Step-up on API and SDK sign-in</b></h2><p>Managed login covers browser-based flows, including federation, but plenty of clients authenticate directly against the Amazon Cognito API, and the same assurance model applies there, such as using the <code>USER_AUTH</code> flow with the <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_InitiateAuth.html\">InitiateAuth</a></span> API. This matters for a mobile app, a desktop client, or a service that drives sign-in programmatically and needs to raise assurance before a sensitive call.</p><p>Take a mobile trading app that signs its users in with the SDK. A user opens the app with a password and reaches level 1, which is enough to browse watchlists and market data. When they place a trade, the app’s policy requires level 4. Because the app authenticates through the API rather than a browser redirect, it starts a new <code>USER_AUTH</code> flow and passes the access token it already holds along with <code>TARGET_ACR_VALUES</code>, requesting the level it needs. Amazon Cognito drives the user through a factor combination that satisfies level 4, then returns tokens whose <code>acr</code> claim the app validates before it submits the order. The <code>InitiateAuth</code> request would look similar to the following API call.</p><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">{\n  \"AuthFlow\": \"USER_AUTH\",\n  \"ClientId\": \"abc123def456\",\n  \"AuthParameters\": {\n    \"USERNAME\": \"user@example.com\",\n    \"ACCESS_TOKEN\": \"eyJraWQiOi...\",\n    \"TARGET_ACR_VALUES\": \"urn:cognito:loa:4\",\n    \"MAX_AGE\": \"300\"\n  }\n}</code></pre><p>Passing the access token the app already holds lets Amazon Cognito step the user up from the factors in that token; without it, Amazon Cognito authenticates the user from scratch. The <code>MAX_AGE</code> parameter behaves the same way it does on managed login. It ties the requirement to <code>auth_time</code>, so a trade placed after the window elapses forces a fresh authentication even if the app refreshed its tokens in the meantime. One behavior specific to the API path is worth knowing when you test on a lower Cognito feature plan. If you request step-up through <code>InitiateAuth</code> on a user pool that isn’t on the Essentials or Plus plan, the API returns a <code>FeatureUnavailableInTierException</code>. Managed login handles the same situation differently: the authorize endpoint silently ignores <code>acr_values</code> and proceeds with normal authentication rather than raising an error.</p><h2 id=\"sec-6\"><b>Pattern 4: Carrying assurance across a federated OIDC identity provider</b></h2><p>The three patterns so far assume Amazon Cognito is where the user authenticates and the authoritative source for the identity. Many organizations federate sign-in to a third-party OIDC provider, so the person proving who they are is doing it against an external identity provider (IdP), while Amazon Cognito brokers the session. The assurance model follows them there.</p><p>When your application requests a level, it sends <code>acr_values</code> and optionally <code>max_age</code> to the Amazon Cognito authorize endpoint exactly as before. If that user is configured to federate through an OIDC IdP, Amazon Cognito sends the authorization request to that IdP on the user’s behalf. It translates the level you requested into the <code>acr</code> value the IdP expects, using the mapping you configured, and passes <code>max_age</code> along with it. A standards-compliant OIDC IdP honors <code>acr_values</code> the same way Amazon Cognito does: it drives the user through an authentication that satisfies the requested level, and it returns its own <code>acr</code> value describing what the user reached. <code>max_age</code> carries forward in the same way, so a freshness requirement is enforced at the provider that actually authenticates the user rather than being lost at the broker. Keep in mind that this is only supported with OIDC identity providers.</p><p>The request is unchanged from the application’s point of view. It still directs to Amazon Cognito:</p><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">GET https://auth.example.com/oauth2/authorize\n  ?response_type=code\n  &amp;client_id=abc123def456\n  &amp;redirect_uri=https://app.example.com/callback\n  &amp;scope=openid\n  &amp;identity_provider=PartnerOIDC\n  &amp;acr_values=urn:cognito:loa:3\n  &amp;max_age=300</code></pre><p>The interaction gains a third participant, the downstream IdP, but the assurance requirement is persisted through the entire federated authentication flow as shown in figure 2.</p><p>\n <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/b03191682a3c5f3f.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/3e1c005c4780302a.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/6291d6112039d070.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/b03191682a3c5f3f.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Figure 2: Federation with an OIDC third-party IdP, persisting the acr_values and max_age request parameters\" width=\"1198\" height=\"1063\">\n </p><p>Figure 2: Federation with an OIDC third-party IdP, persisting the acr_values and max_age request parameters</p><p>The value that comes back from a partner IdP won’t use Amazon Cognito’s level URIs, so this lets you map the provider’s <code>acr</code> values onto its own levels with the <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito-user-identity-pools/latest/APIReference/API_UpdateIdentityProvider.html#CognitoUserPools-UpdateIdentityProvider-request-AcrMapping\">AcrMapping</a></span> field on the OIDC IdP configuration. A partner IdP that reports, say, <code>https://partner.example/assurance/strong</code> can be mapped to Amazon Cognito level 3 (<code>urn:cognito:loa:3)</code>, so that by the time the token reaches your application the <code>acr</code> claim speaks the same vocabulary regardless of whether the user authenticated locally or through a federated provider. Your resource server keeps a single enforcement rule and authorization logic and doesn’t need to have special handling depending on where the user ultimately authenticated. See <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-oidc-idp.html#cognito-user-pools-step-up-federation\">Step-up authentication for federated users</a></span> to learn how to map external OIDC IdP <code>acr</code> levels to Cognito.</p><p>This is the same pattern that <span><a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9470/\">RFC 9470</a></span>, the OAuth 2.0 Step Up Authentication Challenge Protocol, describes: the assurance level is requested at the edge of your application, travels through Amazon Cognito to whatever provider authenticates the user, and returns normalized to a vocabulary your code already understands. A user federating from a corporate IdP and a user signing in against the same user pool directly both arrive at your resource server carrying an <code>acr</code> claim that means the same thing.</p><h2 id=\"sec-7\"><b>Conclusion</b></h2><p>In this post, I showed you how the <code>acr</code> and <code>amr</code> claims in Amazon Cognito let you match authentication strength to the risk of what a user is doing, instead of settling for one bar at the front door. Your application requests a target level with <code>acr_values</code> and <code>max_age</code> (or <code>TARGET_ACR_VALUES</code> and <code>MAX_AGE</code> in the <code>USER_AUTH</code> flow), Amazon Cognito reports the level reached and the methods used, and your application enforces what each operation requires. The four patterns, from a banking transfer to a federated third-party IdP, follow that same loop, and one enforcement rule at your resource server covers every sign-in path.</p><p>The larger shift is that assurance is now something Amazon Cognito expresses in a standard way and your application reasons about directly, without building your own step-up tracking. To get started, move your user pool to the Essentials or Plus feature plan, decide the level each operation requires, and add <code>acr_values</code> to your existing authorize requests. For the complete parameter reference and federated behavior, see the <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/\">Amazon Cognito Developer Guide</a></span>.</p><p>If you have questions about this post, contact <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/contact-us/\">AWS Support</a> or start a new thread on <a href=\"https://repost.aws/\">AWS re:Post</a>.</p><hr><p> \n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/b21d56a30fc34ebe.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\" width=\"120\" height=\"160\"> \n  </p><h3 id=\"sec-8\">Abrom Douglas III</h3><p>Abrom is a Senior Solutions Architect within AWS Identity with over 20 years of software engineering and security experience, specializing in identity and access management. He loves speaking with customers about how identity and access management can provide secure outcomes that enable both business and technology initiatives. In his free time, he enjoys cheering for Arsenal FC, photography, travel, volunteering, and competing in duathlons.</p>","zhKind","complete","蓝队","加固","AWS","工具发布","https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/step-up-authentication-how-to-right-size-authentication-to-risk/","AWS Security Blog",{"_121":122,"_123":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
